from pathlib import Path
import shutil
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
for name in ['manuscript.html','case_layout.py']:
 dest=ROOT/('before_conserved_mesh_'+name)
 if not dest.exists():shutil.copy2(ROOT/name,dest)
p=ROOT/'manuscript.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
start=s.index('<h3>3.4.');end=s.index('</section>',start)
s=s[:start]+r'''<h3>3.4. A mesh generated by a conserved density</h3>
<p>The reciprocal coordinate explains the geometry of the natural lattice. A cell moving with velocity \(-u\) carries a constant mass of \(\rho\), so equal masses produce wider cells where the density is smaller. For the smooth soliton profiles considered here, these wider cells occur near the velocity peaks. We therefore seek a conserved density that places more nodes in the wave region and supplies a corresponding rule for their motion.</p>
<p>Set \(m=u_{xx}+2\) and \(D=\partial_t-u\partial_x\). Differentiating (1) gives \(Dm=2u_xm+c^2\rho\rho_x\), while the density equation gives \(D\rho=\rho u_x\). Consequently, \(D(m/\rho)=u_xm/\rho+c^2\rho_x\). We obtain the conservation law</p>
\[R_m=\frac{m}{2\rho},\qquad Q_m=-uR_m-\frac{c^2}{2}(\rho-1),\qquad (R_m)_t+(Q_m)_x=0.\tag{15}\]
<p>The constant in the flux sets \(Q_m=0\) at the background state. Wherever \(R_m>0\), the differential \(d\xi=R_m\,dx-Q_m\,dt\) defines a local conservation coordinate. Its fixed labels move with velocity \(V=Q_m/R_m\). For the one-soliton family, writing \(s=1-2/(cp)\) and \(z=\tanh(\theta_1/2)\) gives \(R_m=1+s^2(1-z^2)(1+s^2z^2)/(1-s^2z^2)^2\geq1\). Thus this density increases inside the wave and assigns more nodes there. In the two-soliton experiment we evaluate the same density from the exact initial fields and monitor its positivity during evolution.</p>
<p>We initialize the moving grid by equal increments of accumulated \(R_m\) on \([-4,4]\): \(\int_{-4}^{x_k(0)}R_m(x,0)\,dx=\frac{k}{N-1}\int_{-4}^{4}R_m(x,0)\,dx\). At interior nodes we evolve \(\dot x_k=V_k=-u_k-c^2(\rho_k-1)/(2R_{m,k})\). The two boundary nodes remain fixed and receive exact time-dependent boundary values. The conservation-coordinate velocity is therefore imposed in the interior, with a prescribed boundary closure.</p>
<p>Both mesh choices use the same physical-field equations along the nodes,</p>
\[\dot m=(u+V)m_x+2u_xm+c^2\rho\rho_x,\qquad \dot\rho=(u+V)\rho_x+\rho u_x,\qquad u_{xx}=m-2.\]
<p>We approximate the spatial derivatives with three-point formulas on the current mesh and recover \(u\) through the discrete boundary-value problem. The initial interior values are \(m_k=\mathcal D_2u_*(x_k,0)+2\), so the initial recovery reproduces the sampled exact velocity. Exact boundary values of \(u,\rho,m\) are used at every RK4 stage. Fixed FD uses uniform initial nodes and \(V=0\); conserved-density moving FD uses the equal-mass initialization and the velocity above. Both fields are stored at nodes. This formulation allows node allocation and motion to be examined with the field discretization and boundary data held common.</p>
''' +s[end:]
start=s.index('<h3 id="s44">');end=s.index('<h3 id="s45">',start)
s=s[:start]+r'''<h3 id="s44">4.4. Fixed and conserved-density moving meshes</h3>
<p>The choice of conserved density determines where a moving grid spends its resolution. We examine this effect by comparing uniform fixed FD with the \(R_m\)-based moving FD of Section 3.4. Both calculations use 201 nodes on the physical interval \([-4,4]\), the same boundary data and field-difference formulas, and RK4 with \(\Delta T=5\times10^{-4}\) up to \(T=0.25\). At each intermediate RK4 stage, the moving calculation updates the density and node velocity from the current fields.</p>
<p>We reconstruct both nodal fields by piecewise-linear interpolation on 2,001 common physical points in \([-2.5,2.5]\). The reported errors are the maximum differences from the continuous exact fields at these points. This common reconstruction includes the effect of node placement in the measured field accuracy.</p>
{{FIG_MESH_EVOLUTION}}
<p>In both cases, the conserved-density mesh concentrates resolution within the wave region and reduces the maximum errors of both reconstructed fields. The conservation law supplies an initial allocation through its density and a subsequent motion through its flux. Their combined use turns the geometry of the exact waves into a practical mesh choice, while the common field discretization keeps the comparison focused on that choice.</p>
''' +s[end:]
s=s.replace('Experiments with curvature-adapted initial labels examine how a different allocation of nodes affects the subsequent moving-grid computation.','A second conserved density, derived from the velocity curvature and physical density, generates a mesh that concentrates nodes in the wave region and reduces both field errors in the tested one- and two-soliton evolutions.')
s=s.replace('We then use the conservation coordinate to examine mesh allocation and construct curvature-adapted initial labels for the direct moving-grid formulation.','We then derive a conserved density involving the velocity curvature and use its flux to construct a moving mesh. A matched comparison with uniform fixed FD examines how this allocation affects the two reconstructed fields.')
s=s.replace('Section 4.4 examines mesh allocation and adaptive initial labels.','Section 4.4 compares uniform fixed meshes with conserved-density moving meshes.')
start=s.index('<p>The conservation law provides a second route');end=s.index('</p>',start)+4
s=s[:start]+r'''<p>The conservation laws provide a second route from structure to computation. The physical density explains why the natural equal-mass mesh expands near smooth velocity peaks. The alternative density \(R_m=(u_{xx}+2)/(2\rho)\) instead increases in the wave region, and its flux supplies the corresponding interior node velocity. With common field differences, boundary values and RK4 integration, this mesh reduces both reconstructed field errors in the one- and two-soliton tests. The reductions range from approximately 29% to 43% at the reported resolution and final time. This connects the choice of conserved density with a measurable change in how effectively a fixed node budget resolves the physical fields.</p>''' +s[end:]
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=ROOT/'case_layout.py';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8').replace("data/fd_mesh_comparison.json","data/conserved_mesh.json")
start=s.index('  fixed,natural,adaptive=entries');end=s.index('\n rep.update',start)
s=s[:start]+'''  fixed,adaptive=entries
  reductions=[100*(1-adaptive[q]/fixed[q]) for q in ['u','rho']]
  introduction=['The single wave provides a direct test of the allocation predicted by the conserved density. Figure 7 relates the terminal cell widths to the spatial distribution of the two field errors.','For the two-soliton profile, the same density allocates nodes around both wave structures. Figure 8 shows how this redistribution changes the errors across the common physical interval.'][i]
  observation=['The moving grid has smaller cells around the localized wave and wider cells in the background. Both maximum field errors decrease, with the larger reduction occurring in the velocity.','The nonuniform widths resolve the two wave structures with the same total node count. Both maximum errors decrease by similar proportions, extending the benefit of this density choice to the two-wave profile.'][i]
  meshes.append('<h4>'+['One-soliton case','Two-soliton case'][i]+'</h4><p>'+introduction+'</p>'+tab+fig(i+7,[f'conserved_mesh_{i}'],case+': fixed and conserved-density moving FD at T=0.25. Left: physical cell widths. Center and right: absolute errors after linear reconstruction on common physical points.')+f'<p>{observation} Relative to fixed FD, the reductions are {reductions[0]:.1f}% for velocity and {reductions[1]:.1f}% for density. The minimum moving-cell width during evolution is {adaptive["min_h"]:.4f}, and the computed density and mesh monitor remain positive. Halving the RK4 step changes the terminal nodal states by less than {sci(max(d["dt_halving_change"] for d in entries))}.</p>')
''' +s[end:]
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
p=ROOT/'validate.py';s=p.read_text().replace("'fd_mesh_comparisons':6","'fd_mesh_comparisons':4")
s=s.replace("out={'file'", "mesh=json.loads((ROOT/'data/conserved_mesh.json').read_text())\nassert len(mesh)==4\nassert all(d['min_h']>0 and d['min_rho']>0 and d['min_Rm']>0 and d['dt_halving_change']<1e-10 for d in mesh)\nassert all(len(s.find_all('table')[i].select('tbody tr'))==2 for i in [4,5])\nassert 'curvature-adapted' not in s.get_text().lower()\nout={'file'")
